// The float lane's uniform table: the lane's own derived grid over
// [0, kFlatHiF32), every interval the same width (kFlatWidthF32 =
// 0.125), every order fitted independently at its
// interval's own degree (kFlatDegsF32), at the read cap
// kFlatReadCapF32 = 4 or below.
//
// The grid is the lane's own and not the double table's: it is derived
// from this lane's bound, this lane's format and this lane's read cap,
// so its interval count, its join and its degrees are the double
// lane's only by coincidence.
// Degree 4 is the smallest ClenshawSplit reads - it takes its top
// odd coefficient at c[2m-1] and requires an even degree of at least
// 4 - and half an ulp of 1 in binary32 is 5.96e-8 against this lane's
// 1e-7 bar, so on the first cell - the worst one - the delivered
// figure is 7.747e-8 in the split Clenshaw route and 3.745e-8 in
// Horner, against a truncation bound of 8.436e-9. What this table
// delivers is the lane's rounding and not its truncation, and a
// higher even degree buys nothing for two more coefficients per
// order.
